{"id":3639,"date":"2018-10-14T17:39:48","date_gmt":"2018-10-14T21:39:48","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/?p=3639"},"modified":"2018-10-20T16:48:19","modified_gmt":"2018-10-20T20:48:19","slug":"antwerpen-belgium","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/travel-log\/antwerpen-belgium\/","title":{"rendered":"Antwerpen, Belgium"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The citizens of Antwerpen are a very proud people. If you ask them what is the best thing in Brussels, they will simply say &#8220;the train to Antwerpen&#8221;. The citizens of Antwerpen are also a very tolerant people. With 170 nationalities, they are the most diverse city in Europe after Amsterdam, with 180.<\/p>\n<style type=\"text\/css\">\r\n\t#foogallery-gallery-3526 .fg-image {\r\n        width: 150px;\r\n    }\r\n<\/style>\r\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"foogallery foogallery-container foogallery-default foogallery-lightbox-foobox fg-gutter-10 fg-center fg-default fg-light fg-border-thin fg-shadow-outline fg-loading-default fg-loaded-fade-in fg-hover-fade fg-hover-zoom fg-ready\" id=\"foogallery-gallery-3526\" data-foogallery=\"{&quot;item&quot;:{&quot;showCaptionTitle&quot;:true,&quot;showCaptionDescription&quot;:true},&quot;lazy&quot;:true}\" >\r\n\t<div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4145.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"As you come up the escalators from the platforms down below, this ornate clock wall welcomes you.\" data-attachment-id=\"3545\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"As you come up the escalators from the platforms down below, this ornate clock wall welcomes you.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4145\/2747978084.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">As you come up the escalators from the platforms down below, this ornate clock wall welcomes you.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4162.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Named the fourth most beautiful train station in the world by Newsweek in 2009, The Antwerpen train station stands tall and proud right next to the Diamond district. It was designed by Louis Delacenserie in 1895 and completed in 1905.\" data-attachment-id=\"3548\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Named the fourth most beautiful train station in the world by Newsweek in 2009, The Antwerpen train station stands tall and proud right next to the Diamond district. It was designed by Louis Delacenserie in 1895 and completed in 1905.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4162\/4132468951.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Named the fourth most beautiful train station in the world by Newsweek in 2009, The Antwerpen train station stands tall and proud right next to the Diamond district. It was designed by Louis Delacenserie in 1895 and completed in 1905.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4219.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"84% of all diamond trade passes through Antwerpen. Given the amount of diamonds in the city, the diamond quarter has thousands and thousands of security cameras. So, if you ever feel like you are in danger, the diamond quarter might be the safest place that you can be.\" data-attachment-id=\"3544\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"84% of all diamond trade passes through Antwerpen. Given the amount of diamonds in the city, the diamond quarter has thousands and thousands of security cameras. So, if you ever feel like you are in danger, the diamond quarter might be the safest place that you can be.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4219\/4028518447.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">84% of all diamond trade passes through Antwerpen. Given the amount of diamonds in the city, the diamond quarter has thousands and thousands of security cameras. So, if you ever feel like you are in danger, the diamond quarter might be the safest place that you can be.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4169.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"In the middle of the Grote Markt (i.e. Big Market) stands Brabo&#039;s Monument explaining the origin of the city&#039;s name. Legend says that a giant named Antigoon guarded the Scheldt river. Every merchant that wanted to pass and head to the harbor had to give him half of the goods that he intended to sell. If not, the giant would cut of one of the merchant&#039;s hands and would throw it in the water. One day Brabo passed on the river by the giant and cut off his hand, throwing it to the water as well, thus vanquishing the giant. In Dutch &lt;i&gt;hand werpen&lt;\/i&gt; means &quot;hand throwing&quot; and eventually resulted in Antwerpen. This statue shows Brabo in the process of throwing the giant&#039;s hand and the giant lay at Brabo&#039;s feet.\n&lt;p&gt;The building under renovation behind the statue is Town Hall, started in 1561 and completed in 1564.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"3550\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"In the middle of the Grote Markt (i.e. Big Market) stands Brabo&#039;s Monument explaining the origin of the city&#039;s name. Legend says that a giant named Antigoon guarded the Scheldt river. Every merchant that wanted to pass and head to the harbor had to give him half of the goods that he intended to sell. If not, the giant would cut of one of the merchant&#039;s hands and would throw it in the water. One day Brabo passed on the river by the giant and cut off his hand, throwing it to the water as well, thus vanquishing the giant. In Dutch &lt;i&gt;hand werpen&lt;\/i&gt; means &quot;hand throwing&quot; and eventually resulted in Antwerpen. This statue shows Brabo in the process of throwing the giant&#039;s hand and the giant lay at Brabo&#039;s feet.\n&lt;p&gt;The building under renovation behind the statue is Town Hall, started in 1561 and completed in 1564.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4169\/4245960654.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">In the middle of the Grote Markt (i.e. Big Market) stands Brabo's Monument explaining the origin of the city's name. Legend says that a giant named Antigoon guarded the Scheldt river. Every merchant that wanted to pass and head to the harbor had to give him half of the goods that he intended to sell. If not, the giant would cut of one of the merchant's hands and would throw it in the water. One day Brabo passed on the river by the giant and cut off his hand, throwing it to the water as well, thus vanquishing the giant. In Dutch <i>hand werpen<\/i> means \"hand throwing\" and eventually resulted in Antwerpen. This statue shows Brabo in the process of throwing the giant's hand and the giant lay at Brabo's feet.\n<p>The building under renovation behind the statue is Town Hall, started in 1561 and completed in 1564.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4168.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The houses used to house the richest guilds of the city. The (faux-)gold statues on top served as a way of representing their trade. For example, the one in the middle is of Saint George fighting the dragon, which represents the guild of the bow and arrow. A statue more to the right depicting a fox served to represent the guild of fencing because its members had to be cunning, not necessarily powerful.\" data-attachment-id=\"3549\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The houses used to house the richest guilds of the city. The (faux-)gold statues on top served as a way of representing their trade. For example, the one in the middle is of Saint George fighting the dragon, which represents the guild of the bow and arrow. A statue more to the right depicting a fox served to represent the guild of fencing because its members had to be cunning, not necessarily powerful.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4168\/3259698775.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The houses used to house the richest guilds of the city. The (faux-)gold statues on top served as a way of representing their trade. For example, the one in the middle is of Saint George fighting the dragon, which represents the guild of the bow and arrow. A statue more to the right depicting a fox served to represent the guild of fencing because its members had to be cunning, not necessarily powerful.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4179.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Butcher&#039;s guild was also a very rich guild but it does not stand with the others due to the smell that comes with the job. Instead, it is a couple hundred meters northeast of the Town Hall. Nonetheless, the Vleeshuis (i.e. Meat house in Dutch) didn&#039;t hesitate to show off their wealth. The Vleeshuis is significantly larger than the other guild houses and it is built with alternating white and red stones to give the effect of bacon. Currently, the building is used as a museum describing the life of a butcher during that time.\" data-attachment-id=\"3641\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Butcher&#039;s guild was also a very rich guild but it does not stand with the others due to the smell that comes with the job. Instead, it is a couple hundred meters northeast of the Town Hall. Nonetheless, the Vleeshuis (i.e. Meat house in Dutch) didn&#039;t hesitate to show off their wealth. The Vleeshuis is significantly larger than the other guild houses and it is built with alternating white and red stones to give the effect of bacon. Currently, the building is used as a museum describing the life of a butcher during that time.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4179\/1378837756.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Butcher's guild was also a very rich guild but it does not stand with the others due to the smell that comes with the job. Instead, it is a couple hundred meters northeast of the Town Hall. Nonetheless, the Vleeshuis (i.e. Meat house in Dutch) didn't hesitate to show off their wealth. The Vleeshuis is significantly larger than the other guild houses and it is built with alternating white and red stones to give the effect of bacon. Currently, the building is used as a museum describing the life of a butcher during that time.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4180.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Hetsteen is the oldest building in Antwerp. It was originally used as a fortress and then as a prison by Philip II. In fact, that is the reason why the castle is made out of two different color stones. While Philip II was using it as a prison, he needed more space due to his Spanish Inquisition so he had a second story built. Not only could you not choose whether to go to prison or not, but you were forced to pay to stay there and receive the minimal amount of comfort: 7 cents per night, 2 cents to eat, 1 penny to go to the bathroom and even more if you wanted to bring your own bed in. Needless to say that many people couldn&#039;t afford for even basic comforts and often died in the prison.\n&lt;p&gt;Up until recently, though, the castle was actually used as a youth center for creativity and dance. After the current renovations are complete, it will again change its purpose and will be used by the Office of Tourism instead.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"3527\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Hetsteen is the oldest building in Antwerp. It was originally used as a fortress and then as a prison by Philip II. In fact, that is the reason why the castle is made out of two different color stones. While Philip II was using it as a prison, he needed more space due to his Spanish Inquisition so he had a second story built. Not only could you not choose whether to go to prison or not, but you were forced to pay to stay there and receive the minimal amount of comfort: 7 cents per night, 2 cents to eat, 1 penny to go to the bathroom and even more if you wanted to bring your own bed in. Needless to say that many people couldn&#039;t afford for even basic comforts and often died in the prison.\n&lt;p&gt;Up until recently, though, the castle was actually used as a youth center for creativity and dance. After the current renovations are complete, it will again change its purpose and will be used by the Office of Tourism instead.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4180\/1432655412.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Hetsteen is the oldest building in Antwerp. It was originally used as a fortress and then as a prison by Philip II. In fact, that is the reason why the castle is made out of two different color stones. While Philip II was using it as a prison, he needed more space due to his Spanish Inquisition so he had a second story built. Not only could you not choose whether to go to prison or not, but you were forced to pay to stay there and receive the minimal amount of comfort: 7 cents per night, 2 cents to eat, 1 penny to go to the bathroom and even more if you wanted to bring your own bed in. Needless to say that many people couldn't afford for even basic comforts and often died in the prison.\n<p>Up until recently, though, the castle was actually used as a youth center for creativity and dance. After the current renovations are complete, it will again change its purpose and will be used by the Office of Tourism instead.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4181.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The statue of the giant &#039;Lange Wapper&#039; stands in front of Hetsteen. Legend says that he was the original occupant of the castle. At night, the giant would go out and shape-shift into various living things to scare the drunks roaming around at night. The two tiny figures of this statue represent two terrified drunks at the feet of &#039;Lange Wapper&#039;.\" data-attachment-id=\"3528\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The statue of the giant &#039;Lange Wapper&#039; stands in front of Hetsteen. Legend says that he was the original occupant of the castle. At night, the giant would go out and shape-shift into various living things to scare the drunks roaming around at night. The two tiny figures of this statue represent two terrified drunks at the feet of &#039;Lange Wapper&#039;.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4181\/2023803991.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The statue of the giant 'Lange Wapper' stands in front of Hetsteen. Legend says that he was the original occupant of the castle. At night, the giant would go out and shape-shift into various living things to scare the drunks roaming around at night. The two tiny figures of this statue represent two terrified drunks at the feet of 'Lange Wapper'.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4186.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Given the importance of diamonds to the Antwerpen economy, it is only natural that there is a museum on diamonds in the city.\n&lt;i&gt;Interestingly, the Jewish community has historically been the group that have controlled most of the world&#039;s diamonds. This is logical considering that the Jewish people have often been a persecuted people and have often been deprived of the right to own land. As a result, they have needed to find some way to store their wealth in a way that is easily mobile. The solution: diamonds.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"3529\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Given the importance of diamonds to the Antwerpen economy, it is only natural that there is a museum on diamonds in the city.\n&lt;i&gt;Interestingly, the Jewish community has historically been the group that have controlled most of the world&#039;s diamonds. This is logical considering that the Jewish people have often been a persecuted people and have often been deprived of the right to own land. As a result, they have needed to find some way to store their wealth in a way that is easily mobile. The solution: diamonds.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4186\/3619598086.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Given the importance of diamonds to the Antwerpen economy, it is only natural that there is a museum on diamonds in the city.\n<i>Interestingly, the Jewish community has historically been the group that have controlled most of the world's diamonds. This is logical considering that the Jewish people have often been a persecuted people and have often been deprived of the right to own land. As a result, they have needed to find some way to store their wealth in a way that is easily mobile. The solution: diamonds.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4188.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Various street art can be found hiding in plain sight all around the city\" data-attachment-id=\"3530\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Various street art can be found hiding in plain sight all around the city\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4188\/834765875.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Various street art can be found hiding in plain sight all around the city<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4200.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The entrance to one of the few remaining alleys of the Middle Ages\" data-attachment-id=\"3535\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The entrance to one of the few remaining alleys of the Middle Ages\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4200\/3991818038.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The entrance to one of the few remaining alleys of the Middle Ages<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_4192.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"While this may seem like a teeny tiny alley to us, this is how most of the streets were a long time ago.\" data-attachment-id=\"3531\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"While this may seem like a teeny tiny alley to us, this is how most of the streets were a long time ago.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_4192\/312600304.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">While this may seem like a teeny tiny alley to us, this is how most of the streets were a long time ago.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.githu